  m_TableData:
  - m_Id: 1510115985891328
    m_Localized: "Cesare Lombroso (1835\u20131909) was an Italian physician and criminologist
      whose work profoundly influenced the early development of forensic science.
      \n"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1510280289361920
    m_Localized: The museum
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1510306809946112
    m_Localized: The criminal anthropology museum hosts his laboratory collections,
      research materials, and personal archives, allowing visitors to explore the
      origins of modern criminology.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1510330079944704
    m_Localized: Pseudoscience and controversy
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1510366939488256
    m_Localized: The theories presented by Lombroso are now wildly recognized as
      pseudoscientific and incorrect. The museum encourgages critical thinking about
      Lombroso's theories.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1510424854437888
    m_Localized: Tickets
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1510449139458048
    m_Localized: "Monday to Saturday, 10:00\u201318:00 (last admission at 17:30).\nClosed
      on Sundays.\n"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1510561253203968
    m_Localized: " - <b>Full price:</b> \u20AC5.00\n\n - <b>Reduced:</b> \u20AC3.00"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1510829806100480
    m_Localized: Free entry every Wednesday throughout the year, and for holders
      of the <i>Abbonamento Musei Torino Piemonte</i> and the <i>Torino+Piemonte
      Card</i>.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1511004293341184
    m_Localized: Reduced and free admissions
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1511031820558336
    m_Localized: 'Who qualifies for reduced admission:'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1511066113187840
    m_Localized: "Visitors over 65; visitors aged 10\u201318; university students
      (excluding the University of Turin) with valid student ID; members of ANMS
      and FAI; and groups with a reservation."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1511084433907712
    m_Localized: 'Who qualifies for free admission:'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1511102742044672
    m_Localized: "Children under 10; \n\nStudents and staff of the University of
      Turin;\n\nICOM members; directors and curators of Italian and foreign museums;\n\nSchool
      classes from public institutions of all levels with advance booking; \n\nVisitors
      with disabilities and their companions; \n\nOfficially licensed tour guides;
      personnel of the Italian Ministry of Culture in managerial roles; registered
      journalists; and current municipal, district, and regional administrators of
      Turin and Piedmont, as well as staff of the Experimental Institute for Plant
      Nutrition."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1511122329444352
    m_Localized: Guided Tours
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1511136397139968
    m_Localized: "\u20AC35.00 (1 hour)."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1511183239127040
    m_Localized: Educational services
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1511206299410432
    m_Localized: 
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1511248955482112
    m_Localized: Elementary
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1511270891692032
    m_Localized: "School Year 2025\u201326"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1511319684030464
    m_Localized: Since the contents of the museum may disturb young children, the
      activities are available for <b>grades 4-5 only</b>
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1511400323719168
    m_Localized: Guided tours
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1511418870931456
    m_Localized: "A 60-minute tour designed to encourage careful observation and
      critical thinking.\nCost: \u20AC35 per class (max 25 students)"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1511433576161280
    m_Localized: Educational Workshops
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1511475179462656
    m_Localized: "<b>From Sherlock Holmes to Fingerprints</b>\nA 90-minute hands-on
      workshop exploring investigative methods, experimentation, and the basics of
      forensic observation.\nCost: \u20AC75 per class (max 25 students)\n\nMuseum
      admission is free for all school groups.\nBooking is required for both tours
      and workshops:\nCall 011 6707732 (Mon\u2013Fri, 9:00\u201313:00 / 14:00\u201315:00)
      or email <i>didattica.musei@unito.it</i>"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1511645451427840
    m_Localized: Middle schools
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1511680289316864
    m_Localized: "School Year 2025\u201326"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1511694713528320
    m_Localized: Visits for lower secondary school classes offer an immersive experience
      that combines direct observation of collections with guided analysis and critical
      reflection. Students explore themes such as scientific method, historical context,
      and the relationship between science and society.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1511709863354368
    m_Localized: Guided tours
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1511732172857344
    m_Localized: "A 60-minute tour designed to encourage active questioning, comparison
      of sources, and an interdisciplinary understanding of the museum\u2019s themes.\nCost:
      \u20AC35 per class (max 25 students)"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1511746253135872
    m_Localized: Educational Workshops
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1511789450272768
    m_Localized: "<b>From Sherlock Holmes to Fingerprints</b>\nA 90-minute hands-on
      workshop exploring investigative methods, experimentation, and the basics of
      forensic observation.\nCost: \u20AC75 per class (max 25 students)\n\nMuseum
      admission is free for all school groups.\nBooking is required for both tours
      and workshops:\nCall 011 6707732 (Mon\u2013Fri, 9:00\u201313:00 / 14:00\u201315:00)
      or email <i>didattica.musei@unito.it</i>\n\nActivities available from 10 September
      2025."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1511963157372928
    m_Localized: High schools
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1511998339194880
    m_Localized: "School Year 2025\u201326"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1512014537596928
    m_Localized: "Activities for upper secondary school classes are designed as in-depth,
      critical explorations of the relationship between science, history, society,
      and identity within the museum\u2019s collections."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1512032136896512
    m_Localized: Guided tours
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1512102907387904
    m_Localized: "A 60-minute visit offering a deeper analysis of historical sources
      and museum objects, encouraging critical thinking and connections to civic
      education and the UN 2030 Agenda.\nCost: \u20AC35 per class (max 25 students)"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1512124071845888
    m_Localized: Educational Workshops
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1512151263518720
    m_Localized: "<b>From Sherlock Holmes to Fingerprints</b>\nA 90-minute participatory
      workshop that promotes active learning through hands-on investigation and scientific
      reasoning.\nCost: \u20AC75 per class (max 25 students)\n\nMuseum admission
      is free for all school groups.\nBooking is required for both tours and workshops:\nCall
      011 6707732 (Mon\u2013Fri, 9:00\u201313:00 / 14:00\u201315:00) or email <i>didattica.musei@unito.it</i>\n\nActivities
      available from 10 September 2025."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1512241482997760
    m_Localized: Location
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1512263553425408
    m_Localized: "<b>Via Pietro Giuria 15 \u2013 10126 Turin</b>\nTel. +39 011 6708195
      \u2013 Fax +39 011 6708196\nEmail: museo.lombroso@unito.it"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1512343647854592
    m_Localized: Historical archive
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1512370160050176
    m_Localized: The Museum conserves orignial correspondecy and original manuscripts
      and instruments used by Lombroso, accessible online or in person with booking.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1512388019396608
    m_Localized: 'Museum''s history

'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1512421288615936
    m_Localized: "Cesare Lombroso began collecting anatomical and criminal anthropology
      specimens in 1859. The collection moved several times\u2014Via della Zecca,
      Via Po, Via Michelangelo, and Corso Galileo Galilei\u2014before being partially
      displayed in the 1985 exhibition La scienza e la colpa. In 2001, it found a
      permanent home at Via Giuria 15, within the Palazzo degli Istituti Anatomici,
      alongside the Museums of Human Anatomy, Anthropology & Ethnography, and, since
      2006, the Museum of Fruit, forming a center dedicated to Turin\u2019s scientific
      positivism of the 19th and early 20th centuries."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1810668506882048
    m_Localized: The first room welcomes the visitors with an overview of Lombroso's
      statistical studies throughout Italy of the time.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1811814667558912
    m_Localized: "<b>Carta geografica dell\u2019Italia con distribuzione di dati
      sul \u201Cdifetto di statura\u201D tra i militari (1863-1876).</b>"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1917459513253888
    m_Localized: '<b>Portrait of Cesare Lombroso</b>

      19th-century physician
      whose work shaped the early development of criminal anthropology. '
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1918027161968640
    m_Localized: The room focuses on some of the instruments used by Lombroso to
      study the criminals' bodies as he looked for a correlation between anatomy
      and behaviour.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1919163952570368
    m_Localized: '<b>19th-Century Pendulum Clock</b>

      This small pendulum clock
      is a simple example of the domestic technologies available in the late 1800s.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1921569222336512
    m_Localized: "<b>Anfossi\u2019s Craniographer</b>\nDesigned in the late 19th
      century by Italian physician Carlo Anfossi, this instrument was used to take
      precise measurements of the skull."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1922294769819648
    m_Localized: "<b>Antique Camera</b>\nPhotography played an important role in
      Lombroso\u2019s work, providing visual records he used to support his theories."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1922794718273536
    m_Localized: This room contains both Lombroso's skeleton, many skulls (object
      of his studies), wax reconstructions of faces and many weapons used by the
      criminals.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1923536921337856
    m_Localized: '<b>Skeleton of Cesare Lombroso</b>

      Cesare Lombroso chose
      to donate his own body for scientific study, in line with the positivist principles
      that shaped his work.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1924406333448192
    m_Localized: "<b>Weapon from Lombroso\u2019s Collections<\\b>\nIn the 19th century,
      such items were used as \u201Cevidence\u201D to support theories linking criminal
      behavior to innate traits\u2014ideas now recognized as scientifically unfounded."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1925108338302976
    m_Localized: "<b>Skull from Lombroso\u2019s Collections</b>\nThis skull is part
      of the anthropological materials gathered by Cesare Lombroso in the late 19th
      century as he attempted to link physical traits to criminal behavior\u2014an
      approach now recognized as scientifically invalid and shaped by the biases
      of his time. "
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1925825237131264
    m_Localized: '<b>Wax Facial Cast</b>

      This wax cast reproduces the face
      of an individual studied by Cesare Lombroso. In the 19th century, such casts
      were used to document facial features for anthropological and criminological
      research.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1926171959271424
    m_Localized: This room contains the "Revelation Skull" that first sparked in
      Lombroso the idea of looking for a correlation between criminal behaviour and
      biological evolution.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1929134412963840
    m_Localized: '<b>Revelation Skull</b>

      This skull was studied by Cesare
      Lombroso as part of his theory of atavism, which suggested that criminals represented
      a return to a more primitive human condition proved by deformities of the skulls.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1929421072670720
    m_Localized: The next sections of the museum focuses on patients of the Collegno
      Asylum, as Lombroso used their works to study human psychology
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1931740132712448
    m_Localized: "<b>Dress by Giuseppe Versino</b>\nLombroso and his contemporaries
      studied the personal belongings, creative works, and everyday objects of asylum
      patients as part of a broader attempt to understand \u201Cmadness\u201D through
      physical, behavioral, and material traces."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1932224629350400
    m_Localized: This section focuses on asylum's patients' belongings and creations,
      such as many decorate pots, studied by Lombroso to undersand criminal psychology.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1933352335089664
    m_Localized: '<b>Decorated Pots from Collegno Asylum</b>

      These ceramic
      pots were painted and decorated by patients of the Collegno Asylum. Each object
      reflects the personal expression and creativity of its maker, and has been
      used by Lombroos to llok for correlations between material objects and "insanity".'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1934222913216512
    m_Localized: "<b>Patient\u2019s Shoe</b>\nThis shoe belonged to a patient of
      the Collegno Asylum and is preserved in Lombroso\u2019s collection. It provides
      a tangible connection to the everyday life of the asylum\u2019s residents and
      serves as a reminder of the personal histories behind the museum\u2019s anthropological
      and psychiatric collections."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1934749415809024
    m_Localized: "<b>Hand-Made Playing Card Set</b>\nThis set of hand-crafted playing
      cards was used by Lombroso to study potential links between patients\u2019
      recreational activities and criminal behavior. Each card provides insight into
      the personal habits, creativity, and daily life of the asylum\u2019s residents,
      illustrating how leisure practices were examined as part of Lombroso's criminal
      anthropology research."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1935407690850304
    m_Localized: '<b>Wooden Model of Philadelphia Jail</b>

      This detailed wooden
      model represents the Philadelphia jail, a key reference in 19th-century studies
      of criminal behavior and penitentiary systems. Lombroso and his contemporaries
      used models like this to analyze prison layouts and study how environments
      might influence criminal conduct, reflecting early efforts to connect architecture,
      social conditions, and criminology.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1935849736937472
    m_Localized: "<b>Model of a French Jail Cell</b>\nThis small-scale model reconstructs
      a typical French jail cell from the 19th century. Such models were used by
      Lombroso and other criminologists to study how the design of prison spaces
      might affect inmate behavior, reflecting the period\u2019s interest in the
      relationship between environment and criminality."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1936099583238144
    m_Localized: "<b>Model of a Swedish Jail Cell</b>\nThis small-scale model reconstructs
      a typical Swedish jail cell from the 19th century. Such models were used by
      Lombroso and other criminologists to study how the design of prison spaces
      might affect inmate behavior, reflecting the period\u2019s interest in the
      relationship between environment and criminality."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1935030568394752
    m_Localized: This room contains 3 detailed models of different jails and cells
      of the XIX century. These models can provide interesitng depictions of what
      a prisoner's life were like at the time.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1937201452064768
    m_Localized: A faithful reconstruction of Lombroso's study room. Looks like something
      is missing here...?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1938980147339264
    m_Localized: This gallery primarly contains various information about Lombroso's
      conclusions. Science now consider all of these incorrect, but but this was
      the first try of understaing what it's now Criminology Science.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1940493817126912
    m_Localized: "<b>Gallows</b>\nThese are actual gallows used for public executions
      in the 19th century. Objects like these reflect the criminal justice practices
      of the time and provide context for the study of crime and punishment in Lombroso\u2019s
      era. They serve as a sobering reminder of how legal systems, societal norms,
      and ideas about criminality were historically intertwined."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1941376403546112
    m_Localized: "<b>Native American Outfit of Edgar Laplante</b>\nThis costume belonged
      to Edgar Laplante, an early 20th-century performer who falsely claimed to be
      a Native American chief named \u201CWhite Elk.\u201D The outfit illustrates
      the period\u2019s fascination with exotic personas and theatrical impersonation,
      as well as the ways such performances intersected with the public\u2019s imagination
      of \u201Cthe other.\u201D Preserved in Lombroso\u2019s collection, it offers
      insight into cultural attitudes and the social curiosities after Lombroso's
      death."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  references:
    version: 2
    RefIds: []
